--TEST--
`Core\Response::setStatus` puts no byte in the body, accepted or refused, which is what makes it not one of `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`'s five body members -- spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's five members each write a body and declare a media type. This one
// declares a status and writes nothing, and that difference is the whole
// reason `E0801` does not reach it: a status set beside an `echo` is not two
// writers disagreeing about what the body is.
//
// Three accepted calls, and the output they share is empty.
var $quiet = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(204);
    Core\Response::setStatus(404);
    Core\Response::setStatus(503);
});
echo "three calls wrote [", $quiet, "]\n";

// A refused one is quiet for a stronger reason: the code is checked before it
// is declared, so a refusal leaves neither a byte nor a declaration behind.
// The `catch` inside the callable binds its own name -- repeating an enclosing
// frame's is an ICE in the lowerer today, which the playbook owns.
var $onRefusal = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Core\Response::setStatus(600);
    } catch (LogicError $tooHigh) {
    }
});
echo "a refusal wrote [", $onRefusal, "]\n";

// And a status before a body leaves the body alone: the bytes below are the
// member's, byte for byte, with a status set in front of each.
var $withStatus = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(201);
    Core\Response::text("created");
});
echo "a body after a status is [", $withStatus, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
three calls wrote []
a refusal wrote []
a body after a status is [created]
